WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:07.799
At 8:07, on the morning of January 13th, 2018,
Hawaii residents were woken up by the following

00:00:14.454 --> 00:00:15.725
SEEK IMMEDIATE SHELTER.

00:00:15.725 --> 00:00:20.265
THIS IS NOT A DRILL.”

00:00:20.265 --> 00:00:26.185
Similar alerts interrupted TV and radio stations,
leading many to hide in closets, pull off

00:00:26.185 --> 00:00:31.425
to the side of the road, and even call to
say goodbye to loved ones.

00:00:31.425 --> 00:00:37.565
For the next 38 minutes, 1.4 million people
contemplated, for the first time with any

00:00:37.565 --> 00:00:42.195
gravity, the hopeless inescapability of nuclear
missiles.

00:00:42.195 --> 00:00:48.395
2,300 miles and half an ocean from the nearest
major city, Hawaiians simply have nowhere

00:00:48.395 --> 00:00:50.675
to hide.

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:55.215
But the truth is… neither do any of us.

00:00:55.215 --> 00:01:01.045
Ronald Reagan made this same solemn discovery
over 40 years ago, when he visited the Cheyenne

00:01:01.045 --> 00:01:05.725
Mountain nuclear bunker outside of Colorado
Springs.

00:01:05.725 --> 00:01:10.765
Today the bunker at Cheyenne Mountain is part
of the United States Space Force, the youngest

00:01:10.765 --> 00:01:14.435
of the country’s six branches of armed forces.

00:01:14.435 --> 00:01:19.375
But back then it was run by NORAD, which you
might recognize from its annual tradition

00:01:19.375 --> 00:01:22.445
of tracking Santa’s sleigh.

00:01:22.445 --> 00:01:29.635
NORAD was and is a real organization — the
North American Aerospace Defense Command — and

00:01:29.635 --> 00:01:33.715
it was set up to monitor attacks from the
skies.

00:01:33.715 --> 00:01:39.255
If the Soviets had ever sent a missile hurling
toward San Francisco, it was NORAD, stationed

00:01:39.255 --> 00:01:45.295
here at Cheyenne Mountain, that would see
it coming and alert the president.

00:01:45.295 --> 00:01:51.025
All this amazed Reagan, who, behind a reinforced
steel door, listened to briefings on how the

00:01:51.025 --> 00:01:53.085
system worked.

00:01:53.085 --> 00:01:59.085
Its giant computer screens and Hollywood-reminiscent
flashing maps gave visitors a distinct feeling

00:01:59.085 --> 00:02:01.595
of reassurance.

00:02:01.595 --> 00:02:05.485
Here science fiction was reality.

00:02:05.485 --> 00:02:11.715
But then Reagan asked the commander a simple
question: What could they actually do, besides

00:02:11.715 --> 00:02:16.235
simply retaliate, after the Soviets fired
a missile?

00:02:16.235 --> 00:02:22.575
The answer deeply disturbed him… “nothing”.

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:27.614
When NORAD was created, in the 50s, it was
designed to help intercept bombers.

00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:34.105
But by the time of Reagan’s visit, the primary
threat had shifted to missiles, leaving NORAD

00:02:34.105 --> 00:02:39.505
the task of merely watching, as residents
of Hawaii thought they were, the end of the

00:02:39.505 --> 00:02:40.715
world.

00:02:40.715 --> 00:02:49.785
Few Americans are aware of how true that fact
remains today, 40 years later, in 2022.

00:02:49.785 --> 00:02:54.864
While the enemy has changed more than once
since Reagan’s time, the basic fact has

00:02:54.864 --> 00:03:01.895
not: the United States still does not have
an effective defense against incoming missiles.

00:03:01.895 --> 00:03:06.325
Read between the lines and not even the Department
of Defense claims to have any protection,

00:03:06.325 --> 00:03:11.015
besides the threat of retaliation, against
the likes of Russia or China.

00:03:11.015 --> 00:03:16.455
But what no politician or Pentagon official
will dare say out loud is that we aren’t

00:03:16.455 --> 00:03:22.215
even defended from a much lesser threat like
North Korea or Iran.

00:03:22.215 --> 00:03:27.775
In all technical likelihood, Kim Jong-Un could
flatten any American city of his choice at

00:03:27.775 --> 00:03:30.165
any time.

00:03:30.165 --> 00:03:35.415
While the Hawaii missile alert was sent by
human error, the truth is that had North Korea

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:41.205
launched a mere fraction of its nuclear stockpile,
it’s all but guaranteed that at least one

00:03:41.205 --> 00:03:42.905
would reach its target.

00:03:42.905 --> 00:03:48.916
It could happen by accident, by breach of
protocol, through a misunderstanding, by an

00:03:48.916 --> 00:03:53.915
irrational actor, or, in theory, a terrorist
plot.

00:03:53.915 --> 00:03:59.065
But even fewer Americans are aware of how
many billions we’ve spent trying to change

00:03:59.065 --> 00:04:02.815
this… with very little success.

00:04:02.815 --> 00:04:07.935
The military is no stranger to wasteful white
elephants or bloated budgets — to say the

00:04:07.935 --> 00:04:13.304
least — yet the scale of our missile defense
efforts would surprise even the most hardened

00:04:13.304 --> 00:04:16.144
critic of the Military-Industrial Complex.

00:04:16.144 --> 00:04:22.494
It’s one of the most expensive defense projects
ever undertaken, though it’s produced far

00:04:22.494 --> 00:04:25.994
less than almost anyone is aware of.

00:04:25.994 --> 00:04:30.644
And certainly less than most who are will
admit.

00:04:30.644 --> 00:04:32.565
Sponsored by Brilliant.

00:04:32.565 --> 00:04:40.084
Learn math, science, and computer science
the intuitive way with the link in the description.

00:04:40.084 --> 00:04:46.135
If America was attacked from afar, it would
be with a strategic ballistic missile.

00:04:46.135 --> 00:04:51.735
Strategic, as opposed to tactical, because
it would be launched from a distance.

00:04:51.735 --> 00:04:56.515
And ballistic, rather than cruise, because
it would strike a fixed, predetermined target

00:04:56.515 --> 00:04:59.984
— like a city or military base.

00:04:59.984 --> 00:05:03.514
Its trajectory would follow three distinct
phases:

00:05:03.514 --> 00:05:09.824
First, during the boost phase, a rocket slowly
propels the missile upwards, producing a bright

00:05:09.824 --> 00:05:12.074
plume of exhaust.

00:05:12.074 --> 00:05:17.445
The midcourse phase begins when the rocket
stops burning, allowing the missile to fly

00:05:17.445 --> 00:05:19.035
toward its target.

00:05:19.035 --> 00:05:24.264
Finally, as the powerful force of gravity
pulls it back down to earth, it enters the

00:05:24.264 --> 00:05:26.894
very short terminal phase.

00:05:26.894 --> 00:05:32.524
Now, all else being equal, if you were trying
to shoot down this incoming missile, you’d

00:05:32.524 --> 00:05:35.614
want to do it as early as possible.

00:05:35.614 --> 00:05:39.884
Not only does it travel slowest during the
boost phase, but the burning rocket makes

00:05:39.884 --> 00:05:42.304
it easiest to spot.

00:05:42.304 --> 00:05:47.604
Plus if worse comes to worst and your first
attempt fails, you might have enough time

00:05:47.604 --> 00:05:49.475
to try again.

00:05:49.475 --> 00:05:55.244
Realistically, however, intercepting a ballistic
missile during its boost phase is usually

00:05:55.244 --> 00:05:57.105
just plain impossible.

00:05:57.105 --> 00:06:01.404
Take, for example, North Korea.

00:06:01.404 --> 00:06:08.304
If a Hwasong-15 missile were launched at Boston,
this is roughly the path it would take.

00:06:08.304 --> 00:06:12.204
According to the American Physical Society,
it would have to be intercepted within the

00:06:12.204 --> 00:06:17.794
first 260 seconds, which would place it about
here.

00:06:17.794 --> 00:06:22.764
For our defending missile to get there in
time, it would have to be launched from somewhere

00:06:22.764 --> 00:06:25.484
inside this circle.

00:06:25.484 --> 00:06:30.744
In other words, we would have to permanently
station missiles above or within either North

00:06:30.744 --> 00:06:34.505
Korean, Chinese, or Russian territory.

00:06:34.505 --> 00:06:39.404
So too would the actual collision occur above
one of those three countries.

00:06:39.404 --> 00:06:44.654
Sure, it could be intercepted earlier, allowing
our missiles to be launched from anywhere

00:06:44.654 --> 00:06:50.035
in here, but that would leave mere seconds
for the incoming missile to be detected, for

00:06:50.035 --> 00:06:56.644
the president to be informed, and for a decision
to be made about whether and how to respond.

00:06:56.644 --> 00:07:01.964
If North Korea were kind enough to attack
further South — say, Hawaii or San Francisco

00:07:01.964 --> 00:07:08.325
— American launchers could theoretically
be stationed in South Korea, Japan, or international

00:07:08.325 --> 00:07:09.595
waters.

00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:15.494
But that would leave them well within range
of a quick preemptive attack.

00:07:15.494 --> 00:07:21.334
We do, on the other hand, have examples of
successful midcourse and terminal phase intercepts.

00:07:21.334 --> 00:07:28.234
Israel’s “Iron Dome” has demonstrated
roughly 90% real-world effectiveness and Iraqi

00:07:28.234 --> 00:07:31.734
SCUD missiles were shot down during the Gulf
War.

00:07:31.734 --> 00:07:37.504
We’re pretty good at intercepting short,
medium, and even intermediate-range missiles

00:07:37.504 --> 00:07:42.734
— under about 2,500 kilometers or 1,500
miles.

00:07:42.734 --> 00:07:48.474
Overseas American military bases and several
of our allies like South Korea and Romania

00:07:48.474 --> 00:07:55.384
are relatively well protected from their neighbors,
sometimes even by overlapping defenses.

00:07:55.384 --> 00:07:58.074
Though even they aren’t perfect.

00:07:58.074 --> 00:08:03.986
The Iron Dome’s 90% track record is only
for the 50% of rockets it chooses to engage.

00:08:03.986 --> 00:08:09.764
And the Patriot Missiles used in the Gulf
War were discovered to be far less effective

00:08:09.764 --> 00:08:12.625
than was known at the time.

00:08:12.625 --> 00:08:19.764
But barring some North Korean takeover of
Vancouver or Cancun, America faces no short,

00:08:19.764 --> 00:08:23.514
medium, or intermediate-range threats.

00:08:23.514 --> 00:08:28.146
Any missile that would hit the United States
would be inter-continental — which is a

00:08:28.146 --> 00:08:31.145
whole different ball game.

00:08:31.145 --> 00:08:37.145
These missiles travel up to 100 times further,
7 times faster, and can do a million times

00:08:37.145 --> 00:08:39.225
more damage.

00:08:39.225 --> 00:08:44.025
Not only that but they go way up into the
upper atmosphere.

00:08:44.025 --> 00:08:49.695
Intercepting ICBMs traveling at least 15,000
miles an hour requires hitting a bullet with

00:08:49.695 --> 00:08:52.665
a bullet… in space.

00:08:52.665 --> 00:08:57.575
To really be effective, they even have to
strike the right part of the bullet.

00:08:57.575 --> 00:08:59.005
So…

00:08:59.005 --> 00:09:00.325
can we do it?

00:09:00.325 --> 00:09:05.815
Today, the system that claims to protect all
50 states from intercontinental ballistic

00:09:05.815 --> 00:09:12.375
missiles like those from North Korea is called
“Ground-Based Midcourse Defense” — GMD.

00:09:12.375 --> 00:09:19.955
Of its 18 tests conducted between 1999 and
2018, it failed eight, giving it a success

00:09:19.955 --> 00:09:23.425
rate of just 55%.

00:09:23.425 --> 00:09:29.355
Of those 8 failures, by the way, 6 were for
different reasons, ranging from sensor malfunctions

00:09:29.355 --> 00:09:31.635
to launch failures.

00:09:31.635 --> 00:09:37.855
Not to mention: the Missile Defense Agency’s
definition of “success” is a bit generous.

00:09:37.855 --> 00:09:45.115
For comparison, our mainly sea-based short-range
defense, called Aegis, works about 81% of

00:09:45.115 --> 00:09:46.465
the time.

00:09:46.465 --> 00:09:52.955
THAAD, the land-based short-range defense
we sell to Israel, the UAE, South Korea, and

00:09:52.955 --> 00:09:56.975
Romania, has a 100% track record.

00:09:56.975 --> 00:10:02.544
Clearly, intercontinental ballistic missiles
— the only kind America is vulnerable to

00:10:02.544 --> 00:10:06.795
— are just much, much harder to stop.

00:10:06.795 --> 00:10:12.005
But more telling is how often GMD isn’t
tested.

00:10:12.005 --> 00:10:17.045
These are all the tests actually conducted
by the Missile Defense Agency across all their

00:10:17.045 --> 00:10:23.425
missile defense programs — GMD, THAAD, and
Aegis — whether they were delayed or happened

00:10:23.425 --> 00:10:25.455
on schedule.

00:10:25.455 --> 00:10:32.105
And these are all the times tests have been
postponed, “merged”, or altogether canceled.

00:10:32.105 --> 00:10:38.585
Between 2010 and 19, nearly two-thirds of
tests fit into this category.

00:10:38.585 --> 00:10:43.835
The original plan for the Ground-Based Midcourse
Defense program, specifically, called for

00:10:43.835 --> 00:10:49.265
4 annual tests for 5 consecutive years, but
there have only actually been less than one

00:10:49.265 --> 00:10:51.395
on average.

00:10:51.395 --> 00:10:56.465
Despite repeated failures, testing has, in
fact, slowed down.

00:10:56.465 --> 00:11:01.095
This does not, obviously, inspire confidence.

00:11:01.095 --> 00:11:06.915
The usual government response is: “Hey,
if one missile only has a 55% chance of hitting

00:11:06.915 --> 00:11:12.465
its target, no worries, we’ll just send
3 or 4”, bringing the probability that at

00:11:12.465 --> 00:11:15.205
least one does to upwards of 95%.

00:11:15.205 --> 00:11:16.205
Voila!

00:11:16.205 --> 00:11:17.565
Problem solved.

00:11:17.565 --> 00:11:22.075
But this is nowhere near as true as it sounds.

00:11:22.075 --> 00:11:28.625
And for a very specific historical reason…

00:11:28.625 --> 00:11:34.245
The United States has been trying to intercept
ballistic missiles since the 1960s and George

00:11:34.245 --> 00:11:38.895
W. Bush entered office already wanting to
invest more in the program.

00:11:38.895 --> 00:11:43.085
But 9/11 changed everything.

00:11:43.085 --> 00:11:46.315
Suddenly there was a tremendous sense of urgency.

00:11:46.315 --> 00:11:53.515
In 2002, Bush announced the Ground-Based Midcourse
Defense system — which was then just a prototype

00:11:53.515 --> 00:12:00.795
with no end to development in sight — would
be declared “operational” in 2004.

00:12:00.795 --> 00:12:05.005
It didn’t matter, in his eyes, that the
technology wasn’t ready for prime time,

00:12:05.005 --> 00:12:10.375
because the threat of “rogue states” like
North Korea was simply too pressing.

00:12:10.375 --> 00:12:15.315
To “accomplish” this massive feat in a
mere two years’ time, Bush did something

00:12:15.315 --> 00:12:17.125
entirely unprecedented.

00:12:17.125 --> 00:12:24.755
Normally, the way just about anything is produced
— from toaster ovens to $100 million missiles

00:12:24.755 --> 00:12:29.875
— is that scientists and engineers first
develop the ‘core’ technology, after which

00:12:29.875 --> 00:12:37.015
it’s all packaged together into one inter-connected
product, and finally its produced at scale.

00:12:37.015 --> 00:12:43.315
Now, if you really need that toaster asap,
you might consider cutting some corners by,

00:12:43.315 --> 00:12:48.515
for instance, starting to manufacture it while
the engineers are still putting on some finishing

00:12:48.515 --> 00:12:54.315
touches — what a project manager would call
a “concurrent schedule”.

00:12:54.315 --> 00:12:59.955
But after 9/11, Bush took this hasty approach
a step further.

00:12:59.955 --> 00:13:04.685
Time was of such great importance that he
started producing the missiles while the technology

00:13:04.685 --> 00:13:10.015
was being developed — effectively doing
everything at once.

00:13:10.015 --> 00:13:16.065
As one retired senior military official admitted:
“We took a system that was still in development…

00:13:16.065 --> 00:13:20.745
and it was declared to be ‘operational’
for political reasons.”

00:13:20.745 --> 00:13:25.525
For every other defense project, contractors
have to demonstrate their technology before

00:13:25.525 --> 00:13:29.345
the Pentagon will pay for it — “fly before
you buy”.

00:13:29.345 --> 00:13:32.835
But GMD was exempted.

00:13:32.835 --> 00:13:36.775
What this meant was that when things would
inevitably break, the engineers would have

00:13:36.775 --> 00:13:43.745
go back and disassemble the highly-complex
missiles and try to retrofit solutions.

00:13:43.745 --> 00:13:50.585
All of this is to say: each missile is hand-made,
and many have been deconstructed and re-designed.

00:13:50.585 --> 00:13:55.545
In other words, there’s no way of knowing
each one’s actual odds of success.

00:13:55.545 --> 00:14:01.075
4 missiles might be 4 times more effective
than 1, or they may not.

00:14:01.075 --> 00:14:03.085
We just don’t know.

00:14:03.085 --> 00:14:07.285
What we do know is that independent experts
aren’t very confident…

00:14:07.285 --> 00:14:14.555
In 2012, a study by the National Academy of
Sciences labeled the system “deficient”.

00:14:14.555 --> 00:14:19.865
Ten years later, the American Physical Society
not only called its current capabilities “low”,

00:14:19.865 --> 00:14:25.315
but said they’ll likely continue to be low
for at least “the next 15 years”.

00:14:25.315 --> 00:14:30.305
America’s own Government Accountability
Office calls its testing, quote, “insufficient

00:14:30.305 --> 00:14:35.044
to demonstrate that an operationally useful
defense capability exists”.

00:14:35.044 --> 00:14:39.445
AKA: it doesn’t work.

00:14:39.445 --> 00:14:46.786
Today, 21 years after 9/11, the program continues
to run in “emergency mode”, with the Missile

00:14:46.786 --> 00:14:53.185
Defense Agency tasked with both developing
the technology and supervising its own performance.

00:14:53.185 --> 00:15:00.945
Now, you might argue that despite these shortcomings,
some defense, even just 55%, is much better

00:15:00.945 --> 00:15:02.745
than zero.

00:15:02.745 --> 00:15:05.805
But there are two problems with such an argument.

00:15:05.805 --> 00:15:10.615
First, missile defense is insanely expensive.

00:15:10.615 --> 00:15:17.325
Cost estimates for the GMD system alone vary,
depending on who you ask, between 40 and 90

00:15:17.325 --> 00:15:22.775
billion dollars — making it more expensive
than the Navy’s latest Ford-class carrier.

00:15:22.775 --> 00:15:30.625
That’s an entire thousand-foot-long, nuclear-powered,
completely redesigned aircraft carrier.

00:15:30.625 --> 00:15:37.185
This is the quintessential American prestige
project — a 3,000-crew, 100-aircraft floating

00:15:37.185 --> 00:15:43.235
military base that makes most countries look
weak by comparison — and yet it was cheaper

00:15:43.235 --> 00:15:47.685
than this one (ineffective) missile defense
system.

00:15:47.685 --> 00:15:53.715
Add up all of the country’s such programs
and the total cost is closer to 350 billion

00:15:53.715 --> 00:15:56.285
current inflation-adjusted dollars.

00:15:56.285 --> 00:16:02.885
That’s an insane amount of money even by
American military standards.

00:16:02.885 --> 00:16:07.015
But the second reason is far, far worse.

00:16:07.015 --> 00:16:12.625
America’s real “Missile Defense Problem”
is not actually that it doesn’t work or

00:16:12.625 --> 00:16:17.785
even that it’s stupidly expensive, but something
else entirely.

00:16:17.785 --> 00:16:22.365
Something so big it deserves its own video.

00:16:22.365 --> 00:16:26.965
Because we’ve only just scratched the surface
of what should be a scandal.

00:16:26.965 --> 00:16:31.255
We certainly haven’t done the science or
engineering behind missile defense justice

00:16:31.255 --> 00:16:36.415
— which is why, in the meantime, you should
check out Brilliant’s hands-on explanations

00:16:36.415 --> 00:16:41.275
on orbits, rockets, gravitational physics,
and so much more.

00:16:41.275 --> 00:16:46.685
These interactive lessons teach you real,
useful math, science, and engineering by explaining

00:16:46.685 --> 00:16:50.905
the answers to questions like “How much
fuel does a rocket need?”, “Why are some

00:16:50.905 --> 00:16:57.185
objects in space spherical and others potato-shaped?”,
and “What is the mathematically optimal

00:16:57.185 --> 00:16:59.775
battery size for a drone?”

00:16:59.775 --> 00:17:03.444
Brilliant is what I desperately wish school
was like.

00:17:03.444 --> 00:17:08.604
They get that you’re naturally going to
learn more when you actually enjoy the process.

00:17:08.604 --> 00:17:11.005
It’s really just common sense.

00:17:11.005 --> 00:17:14.724
It’s designed around the student, not the
teacher.

00:17:14.724 --> 00:17:19.704
You pick whether to start with, for example,
Algebra or Quantum Mechanics, and you choose

00:17:19.704 --> 00:17:24.394
when and where to learn — even if it’s
just a few minutes a day on your phone on

00:17:24.394 --> 00:17:26.865
the way to work or school.

